### 1. Sprint Objective

The main objective of Sprint 1 was to select a NASA Space Apps Challenge, understand its scientific background, identify an environmental problem, and conduct preliminary academic research to develop a potential solution.

### 2. Activities Completed

- Reviewed the available NASA Space Apps Challenge topics.
- Selected the challenge "Dancing with SAR".
- Analyzed the challenge requirements and its scientific objectives.
- Investigated the NASA-ISRO Synthetic Aperture Radar (NISAR) mission and SAR remote sensing technology.
- Conducted preliminary academic research on coastal groundwater systems and satellite-based Earth observation.
- Identified Submarine Groundwater Discharge (SGD) as the main scientific phenomenon underlying our proposed project.
- Investigated how groundwater from coastal aquifers naturally flows through the seabed into the ocean.
- Explored the environmental importance of submarine groundwater discharge in coastal ecosystems and freshwater resource management.
- Discussed the potential of satellite-derived data to identify areas that may indicate groundwater discharge.

### 3. Scientific Background and Problem Identification

Submarine Groundwater Discharge (SGD) is a natural process in which groundwater from coastal aquifers enters the ocean through the seabed.

Although this phenomenon plays an important role in coastal water systems, identifying potential discharge locations and estimating their contribution to coastal environments can be challenging.

Our team identified the need for an accessible, data-driven approach to investigate these processes and support more sustainable coastal groundwater management.

